window.KB_BODY[10]={"102-000-006-om":"<h3>General Information</h3><p>If the engine will out of service in less than 12 months, special precautions <b>must</b> be taken. Follow the short term storage procedure in the Troubleshooting and Repair Manual for the CENTRY™ Electronic Control System, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666070\">3666070</a>, or see the GTA38, K38, K50, QSK38, and QSK50 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a>. If further assistance is required, contact the nearest Cummins® Authorized Repair Location for additional information.</p>","102-000-006-tr-gc":"<h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><p>This procedure describes the proper method for the short term storage (1 to 12 months) of an engine.</p><ul><li>Operate the engine until the coolant temperature is 70°C [160°F].</li><li>Shut the engine down.</li><li>Drain the oil pan sump and oil filters.</li><li>Install the oil pan drain plugs. The oil pan can remain empty until the engine is ready to be put back into service.</li><li>Close the manual fuel shutoff valve.</li></ul><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It is <b>not</b> necessary to drain the coolant if it is a permanent type antifreeze with rust inhibitor.</p></div></div><ul><li>Drain the coolant.</li><li>Put a warning tag on the engine controls. The tag <b>must</b> read &quot;The engine does <b>NOT</b> contain oil. Do <b>NOT</b> operate the engine.&quot;</li><li>Store the engine in an area that is dry and has a uniform temperature.</li><li>Bar the crankshaft two or three revolutions every 2 to 4 weeks.</li></ul><h3>Remove</h3><p>This procedure describes the method for the removal of an engine from short term storage (1 to 6 months).</p><ul><li>Prime the lubricating oil system.</li><li>Fill the oil pan sump.</li><li>Change the fuel filter.</li><li>Fill the coolant system, if necessary.</li><li>Check the power supplies and control system batteries.</li><li>Operate the pre-lube pump.</li><li>Operate the coolant heaters, if installed.</li><li>Start the engine. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-000-002-tr\">Refer to Procedure 000-002</a> in Section 0. Refer to Procedure 007-037 in Section 7.</li></ul>","102-001-026":"<h3>General Information</h3><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00r00990.png\" alt=\"00r00990.png\"></figure><p>Left Bank</p><ol><li>Cam cover mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Cam follower assembly mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Block heater coolant passage capscrew holes</li><li>Coolant passage for block heaters</li><li>Coolant passage to the air compressor</li><li>Electronic control valve assembly mounting capscrew holes and fuel drillings</li><li>Main bearing cap side capscrew mounting holes</li><li>Hand hole cover mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Hand hole cover holes</li><li>Fuel filter head mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Idler shaft oil rifles</li><li>Fuel pump oil supply blanking plate capscrew holes</li><li>Fuel pump oil supply passage.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600281.png\" alt=\"01600281.png\"></figure><p>Right Bank</p><ol><li>Cam cover mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Cam follower assembly mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Block heater coolant passage capscrew holes</li><li>Coolant passage for block heaters</li><li>Coolant passage to the air compressor</li><li>Electronic control valve assembly mounting capscrew holes and fuel drillings</li><li>Oil filter head mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Oil rifle from oil pump to oil filter head to main oil rifle</li><li>Water pump outlet connection mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Idler drillings oil passages</li><li>Main bearing cap side capscrew mounting holes</li><li>Hand hole cover mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Hand hole cover holes</li><li>Water pump mounting location</li><li>Idler shaft oil rifles.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600282.png\" alt=\"01600282.png\"></figure><p>Vee Cavity, QSK45</p><ol><li>Water jacket header plate mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Oil supply to oil coolers</li><li>Water manifold holes used in manufacturing of block</li><li>Oil return from oil coolers</li><li>Idler shaft oil rifle</li><li>Thermostat housing mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Coolant from oil cooler cavity</li><li>Water flow from cooling passages around liners to thermostat housing.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600247.png\" alt=\"00600247.png\"></figure><p>Vee Cavity, QSK60</p><ol><li>Water jacket header plate mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Oil supply to oil coolers</li><li>Water manifold holes used in manufacturing of block</li><li>Oil return from oil coolers</li><li>Idler shaft oil rifle</li><li>Thermostat housing mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Coolant from oil cooler cavity</li><li>Water flow from cooling passages around liners to thermostat housing.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600506.png\" alt=\"01600506.png\"></figure><p>Cylinder Block (Front)</p><ol><li>Front gear housing mounting capscrew holes (M16 x 2.5)</li><li>Camshaft thrust washer mounting capscrew holes (M10 x 1.5-6)</li><li>Camshaft bore (112 ± 0.025 mm [4.409 ± 0.001 in])</li><li>Coolant passages (28.56 ± 0.038 mm [1.124 ± 0.001 in])</li><li>Camshaft oil passages (28.56 ± 0.038 mm [1.124 ± 0.001 in])</li><li>Front cover dowel hole (11.964 mm [0.471 in])</li><li>Idler oil rifle (10 mm [0.394 in])</li><li>Idler oil rifle (10 mm [0.394 in])</li><li>Idler gear retaining capscrew holes (M16 x 2.16)</li><li>Main oil rifle from oil coolers (41.5 ± 0.038 mm [1.634 ± 0.001 in])</li><li>Main oil rifle supply</li><li>Main oil rifle to oil coolers (41.5 ± 0.038 mm [1.634 ± 0.001 in])</li><li>Idler gear shaft bore (22 ± 0.025 mm [0.866 ± 0.001 in]).</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600284.png\" alt=\"01600284.png\"></figure><p>Cylinder Block (Rear)</p><ol><li>Flywheel housing mounting capscrew holes (M16 x 2.6 and M10 x 1.5-6)</li><li>Coolant passages (26 mm [1.024 in])</li><li>Flywheel alignment dowel holes</li><li>Main oil rifle to oil cooler (38 mm [1.496 in])</li><li>Main rifle from oil cooler (38 mm [1.496 in])</li><li>Oil rifle blanking plate capscrew holes (M6 x 1.5)</li><li>Camshaft bore (112 ± 0.025 mm [4.409 ± 0.001 in])</li><li>Oil return drilling (8 mm [0.315 in])</li><li>Piston cooling nozzle rifles.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600250.png\" alt=\"01600250.png\"></figure><p>Cylinder Head Face, QSK45</p><ol><li>Cylinder head retaining capscrew holes (M16 x 1.5 - 4H5H)</li><li>Cylinder head locating dowel holes</li><li>Fuel passages</li><li>Water manifold holes used in manufacturing of block</li><li>Coolant passages</li><li>Turbocharger oil drain holes</li><li>Push rod holes</li><li>Camshaft oil rifle</li><li>Turbocharger oil drain mounting capscrew holes (M10 x 1.5)</li><li>Oil passages to overhead</li><li>Lifting bracket mounting holes.</li></ol><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00600248.png\" alt=\"00600248.png\"></figure><p>Cylinder Head Face, QSK60</p><ol><li>Cylinder head retaining capscrew holes (M6 x 1.5 - 4H5H)</li><li>Cylinder head locating dowel holes</li><li>Fuel passages</li><li>Water manifold holes used in manufacturing block</li><li>Coolant passages</li><li>Turbocharger oil drain holes</li><li>Push rod holes</li><li>Camshaft oil rifle</li><li>Turbocharger oil drain mounting capscrew holes (M10 x 1.5)</li><li>Oil passages to overhead</li><li>Lifting bracket mounting capscrew holes</li><li>Cam follower oil feed drillings.</li></ol><h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><ul><li>Remove the engine. Refer to Procedure 000-001 in Section 0.</li><li>Remove the cylinder head. Refer to Procedure 002-004 in Section 2.</li><li>Remove the gear housing. Reference the following procedure in the QSK45 and QSK60 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021530\">4021530</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/56-001-033-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-033 in Section 1.</a> Reference the following procedure in the QSK45G and QSK60G Industrial Series Engine, Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin 4021383, and the QSK60G Generator CM700 Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin 4021396. Refer to Procedure 001-033 in Section 1.</li><li>Remove the crankshaft. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</li><li>Remove the cylinder liners. Refer to Procedure 001-028 in Section 1.</li><li>Inspect or remove the camshaft bushing. Refer to Procedure 001-010 in Section 1.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure><h3>Clean and Inspect for Reuse</h3><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use care when cleaning the cylinder block. Machined gasket surfaces and camshaft bushings can be damaged.</p></div></div><p>Use a scraper, wire brush, or Scotch-Brite™ pad to clean the heavy dirt deposits from the cylinder block:</p><ul><li>Gasket surfaces</li><li>Mounting surfaces</li><li>Cylinder liner counterbore ledge and the press fit areas</li><li>Cylinder liner packing ring bore</li><li>Top of the cylinder block</li><li>Main bearing saddles and caps</li><li>Cup plug bores.</li></ul><p>Use a bottle brush with a long handle to clean all of the oil passages.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck6bdwa.png\" alt=\"ck6bdwa.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids, or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Make sure the cleaning solution will not damage the camshaft bushings or the AVSEAL® sleeves.</p></div></div><p>Remove the cylinder block from the engine stand and place it in a cleaning tank.</p><p>Follow the instructions of the manufacturer of the cleaning tank and the manufacturer of the cleaning solution.</p><p>Cummins Inc. does <b>not</b> recommend any specific cleaning solution.</p><p>The best results are obtained using a cleaning solution which can be heated to 82 to 93°C [180 to 200°F].</p><p>Cleaning tanks that filter and mix the cleaning solution produce the best results.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck6bdea.png\" alt=\"ck6bdea.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using a steam cleaner, wear safety glasses or a face shield, as well as protective clothing. Hot steam can cause serious personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To reduce the possibility of cylinder block damage, make sure all of the water is removed from the capscrew holes and oil passages.</p></div></div><p>Remove the cylinder block from the cleaning tank.</p><p>Clean the cylinder block with a steam cleaner. Make sure all of the oil passages are clean.</p><p>Dry the cylinder block with compressed air.</p><p>If the cylinder block is <b>not</b> going to be reused immediately, apply a coating of preservative oil to prevent the formation of rust.</p><p>Cover the cylinder block to prevent dirt from sticking to the oil.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck6bdeb.png\" alt=\"ck6bdeb.png\"></figure><h3>Inspect</h3><p>Main Bearing Bore</p><p>Inspect the main bearing bore saddle area for discoloration or damage due to high temperature.</p><p>Discoloration from high temperature indicates the need for a thorough inspection. Repair the main bearing bore, if necessary. Reference the Machine section in this procedure.</p><p>Check the minimum hardness of the cylinder block in the heat affected area.</p><p>Minimum hardness of the cylinder block should be 179HB or above.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When conducting a hardness test, be sure to use a maximum of five dents per test area, as an excessive number of dents will result in irreparable block damage.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb900sc.png\" alt=\"mb900sc.png\"></figure><p>Use crack detection kit, Part Number 3375432, or equivalent, to check the main bore saddle area for cracks.</p><p>If cracks are found, follow the guidelines below.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600523.png\" alt=\"01600523.png\"></figure><p>The guidelines for acceptable cracks are as follows.</p><ul><li>Circumferential cracks that are no longer than 19.050 mm [3/4 in]</li><li>Up to 6 cracks 9.525 mm [3/8 in] and shorter in the saddle area of the bore</li><li>Porosity pockets less than 2.28 mm [0.090 in] diameter.</li></ul><p>If any of the main bearing bores contain any of these criteria, the bearing bore <b>must</b> be repaired. Reference the repair procedure in the Machine section in this procedure.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600524.png\" alt=\"01600524.png\"></figure><p>The guidelines for unacceptable cracks are as follows:</p><ul><li>Cracks entering the oil hole</li><li>Cracks in the radii of the structural webs</li><li>Cracks that break over the side of the main bearing bore saddle.</li></ul><p>If any main bearing bores contain <b>any</b> of these cracks, the block can <b>not</b> be repaired and <b>must</b> be replaced.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If a block has suffered from a spun bearing that has resulted in excessive heat in the main bearing saddle in the location of the internal fuel drillings, it is recommended to <b>not</b> reuse the block. This is due to the susceptibility of a crack at this location that can <b>not</b> be identified using normal test methods.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600525.png\" alt=\"01600525.png\"></figure><p>Check the main bearing caps that are loose. The main bearing cap <b>must</b> be replaced if it is <b>not</b> 0.000 to 0.25 mm [0.000 to 0.01 in] larger than the block.</p><p>New main bearing caps do <b>not</b> have the bore machined to a final specification. If a main bearing cap is replaced, the main bearing bore <b>must</b> be machined. Reference the Machine section in this procedure.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck6brsn.png\" alt=\"ck6brsn.png\"></figure><p>Install the main bearing caps and tighten to specification. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</p><p>Slide the checking ring into the main bearing bore. If the checking ring does <b>not</b> slide through the bore, check the bore for burrs.</p><p>If the checking ring still does <b>not</b> slide through the bore, the bore is undersize and <b>must</b> be repaired.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part Description</th><th>Part Number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Centering Ring</td><td>3164873</td></tr><tr><td>Checking Ring</td><td>3164872</td></tr><tr><td>Checking Bar</td><td>3375061</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb4brdb.png\" alt=\"mb4brdb.png\"></figure><p>Use a 0.076 mm [0.003 in] feeler gauge that is a maximum of 13 mm [0.5 in] wide.</p><p>Center the checking ring in the bore. Attempt to put the feeler gauge between the checking ring and the bore. Rotate the feeler gauge in the bore at both sides of the checking ring.</p><p>The main bearing bore alignment is acceptable if:</p><ul><li>The gauge does <b>not</b> enter at any point</li><li>The gauge will enter, but will <b>not</b> slide through or around the bore, and the alignment bar will rotate with the gauge inserted.</li></ul><p>The main bearing bore alignment is <b>not</b> acceptable if:</p><ul><li>The gauge enters and slides around the bore. (The bore is oversize and <b>must</b> be machined.)</li><li>The gauge will enter on one side <b>only</b>, but can slide around the bore. (The bore is tapered and <b>must</b> be machined.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb4brdc.png\" alt=\"mb4brdc.png\"></figure><p>If the tools to check the main bearing bore alignment are <b>not</b> available, use a bore dial indicator.</p><p>Measure the inside diameter in the three positions indicated in the illustration.</p><p>Make sure the weight of the bore dial indicator does <b>not</b> interfere with the measurement. Keep the solid stem of the indicator against the wall of the bore.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Main Bearing Bore Inside Diameter (Capscrews Tightened to Specifications)</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>192.901</td><td>MIN</td><td>7.594</td></tr><tr><td>192.951</td><td>MAX</td><td>7.596</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The inside diameter of each bore <b>must</b> be completely round within 0.013 mm [0.0005 in].</p><p>If the main bearing bore alignment is <b>not</b> within specifications, the main bearing bore <b>must</b> be machined. Reference the Machine section in this procedure.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck6brsm.png\" alt=\"ck6brsm.png\"></figure><p>Camshaft Bore</p><p>Inspect the camshaft bushing and bores for reuse. Refer to Procedure 001-010 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck6brsi.png\" alt=\"ck6brsi.png\"></figure><p>Head Deck Surface</p><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids, or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><p>Use a scraper or a fibrous, abrasive pad, such as Scotch-Brite™ 7477, or equivalent, and solvent. Clean the top deck surface of the block. Do <b>not</b> allow <b>any</b> dirt into the cylinder. The surface <b>must</b> be free of dirt, oil, and gasket material, but does <b>not</b> have to appear like new metal.</p><p>Check the top surface of the block for wear. If fretting damage is present in the area where a head gasket seal ring or a grommet makes contact, the surface <b>must</b> be repaired. Reference the Machine section in this procedure.</p><p>Fretting damage in any other area is acceptable <b>only</b> if it does <b>not</b> change the protrusion measurement of the counterbore or liner.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02600014.png\" alt=\"02600014.png\"></figure><p>Fretting or pitting of the cylinder block head deck is <b>not</b> uncommon. It is <b>only</b> damaging to the head gasket joint if the fretting or pitting is close to the coolant or oil passages. These areas, if damaged, prevent the head gasket from sealing.</p><p>If the block is damaged, the cylinder block head deck <b>must</b> be machined. Reference the Machine section in this procedure.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02600049.png\" alt=\"02600049.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the dowel pins for damage.</p><p>Use the dowel pin extractor, Part Number ST-1134, or equivalent, to remove the pins if they are damaged.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01400279.png\" alt=\"01400279.png\"></figure><p>Use a precision straight edge (17) to check the head deck of the cylinder block for wear.</p><p>If fretting is visible in an area where a head gasket seal ring or a grommet touches the surface, the surface <b>must</b> be repaired.</p><p>Fretting damage in any other area is acceptable if it does <b>not</b> reduce the ability to measure the liner protrusion accurately.</p><p>Newly machined surfaces <b>must</b> be flat within 0.51 mm [0.020 in] under a cylinder head.</p><p>Waves on the surface are acceptable as long as they are <b>not</b> more than 0.018 mm [0.0007 in] in height. The high and low points of the waves <b>must not</b> be any closer than 25.4 mm [1.0 in].</p><p>If the top surface of the block is machined, the ledge depth of the cylinder liner counterbore <b>must</b> be machined.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck6bdta.png\" alt=\"ck6bdta.png\"></figure><p>Check the top deck surface to make sure it is parallel and perpendicular to the main bearing bore.</p><p>If it is <b>not</b>, the top deck will require resurfacing.</p><p>Reference the Machine section for specifications.</p><p><b>Any</b> main bearing bore repairs <b>must</b> be completed before resurfacing the top deck surface.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600527.png\" alt=\"01600527.png\"></figure><p>Check the concentricity reading of the cylinder bores. Refer to Procedure 001-027 in Section 1. Compare against the flatness of the top deck surface.</p><p>A comparison of these values at 90-degree increments indicates the amount the bores and top deck surface are out of position with each other.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600528.png\" alt=\"01600528.png\"></figure><p>An oversize head gasket is often required on blocks that have had material removed from the top surface of the block.</p><p>Check the block for markings which indicate an oversize gasket is required due to prior salvage machining operations. These markings are often on the front left of the cylinder block head deck surface.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02600015.png\" alt=\"02600015.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the cylinder block to determine which head gasket size is required.</p><p>The parting line of the main bearing cap is <b>not</b> the same as the center line of the main bearing bore.</p><p>Measure the cylinder block height.</p><p>Standard Block Height:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>From crankshaft center line (A):</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>529.525</td><td>MIN</td><td>20.8474</td></tr><tr><td>529.675</td><td>MAX</td><td>20.8533</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>From crankshaft bore (B):</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>433.062</td><td>MIN</td><td>17.0497</td></tr><tr><td>433.212</td><td>MAX</td><td>17.0556</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Block Deck Height from Crankshaft Center Line (A) for Oversize Head Gaskets:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>For +0.25 mm [0.010 in] oversize gasket:</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>529.275</td><td>MIN</td><td>20.8376</td></tr><tr><td>529.425</td><td>MAX</td><td>20.8435</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>For +0.50 mm [0.020 in] oversize gasket:</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>529.025</td><td>MIN</td><td>20.8278</td></tr><tr><td>529.175</td><td>MAX</td><td>20.8337</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>For +0.76 mm [0.030 in] oversize gasket:</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>528.765</td><td>MIN</td><td>20.8175</td></tr><tr><td>528.915</td><td>MAX</td><td>20.8234</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Block Height from Crankshaft Bore (B) for Oversize Head Gaskets:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>For +0.25 mm [0.010 in] oversize gasket:</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>432.812</td><td>MIN</td><td>17.0398</td></tr><tr><td>432.962</td><td>MAX</td><td>17.0457</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>For +0.50 mm [0.020 in] oversize gasket:</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>432.562</td><td>MIN</td><td>17.0300</td></tr><tr><td>432.712</td><td>MAX</td><td>17.0359</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>For +0.76 mm [0.030 in] oversize gasket:</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>432.302</td><td>MIN</td><td>17.0198</td></tr><tr><td>432.452</td><td>MAX</td><td>17.0257</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The height of the cylinder block <b>must not</b> vary more than 0.08 mm [0.003 in] from end to end of the cylinder block. If the cylinder block height is <b>not</b> within specifications, the top surface of the cylinder block <b>must</b> be machined or the block <b>must</b> be replaced.</p><p>If the head deck surface of the block is machined, the ledge depth of the cylinder liner counterbore <b>must not</b> vary more than 0.13 mm [0.0005 in] between the banks.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600529.png\" alt=\"01600529.png\"></figure><p>The cylinder bore angle does <b>not</b> need to be measured, but <b>must</b> be maintained at 30 degrees if the cylinder head surface is machined.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck6bdtc.png\" alt=\"ck6bdtc.png\"></figure><p>Front Gear Train</p><p>Measure the inside diameter of the bore for all four idler shafts.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Idler Shaft Bore Internal Diameter</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>21.975</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.865</td></tr><tr><td>22.025</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.867</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>There is <b>no</b> Cummins Inc. approved salvage to repair the idler shaft bore.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600507.png\" alt=\"01600507.png\"></figure><p>Counterbore</p><p>Inspect the upper cylinder counter bore and lower packing ring bore for reuse. Refer to Procedure 001-027 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600397.png\" alt=\"01600397.png\"></figure><h3>Machine</h3><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Only a competent machinist should attempt these repairs with a fully equipped machine shop and/or using the service tools described in each process. The cylinder block could be irreversibly damaged and therefore not be reused.</p></div></div><p>It is important that each procedure is read before beginning <b>any</b> repairs.</p><p>Make sure all required tools and parts are available before beginning repairs.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600531.png\" alt=\"01600531.png\"></figure><p>Main Bearing Bore</p><p>To repair the main bearing bore, a repair sleeve <b>must</b> be inserted into the bore to bring the bore back to original specifications. The main bearing bore <b>must</b> be machined larger to accept the repair sleeve.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600532.png\" alt=\"01600532.png\"></figure><p>Select the correct repair sleeve from the main bearing saddle inserts table, Part Number 3375168. Consult the Service Products Catalog, Bulletin 3377710.</p><p>Measure the thickness of the repair sleeve. The nominal thickness of a Cummins® service repair sleeve is 2.54 mm [0.100 in]. The sleeve should measure within ± 0.25 mm [± 0.010 in] of this nominal thickness.</p><p>If the thickness of the sleeve is greater than this tolerance, the bore dimensions described in this procedure <b>do not</b> apply. Specifications for thicker sleeves are <b>not</b> addressed in this procedure.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600533.png\" alt=\"01600533.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><p>Put the cylinder block on an adequately sized vibration-resistant work surface.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600534.png\" alt=\"01600534.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This component or assembly weighs greater than 23 kg [50 lb]. To prevent serious personal injury, be sure to have assistance or use appropriate lifting equipment to lift this component or assembly.</p></div></div><p>Position the cylinder block to permit the use of a steady rest during the machining operation.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600535.png\" alt=\"01600535.png\"></figure><p>The cylinder block <b>must</b> sit squarely (flat) on the vibration-resistant work surface.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600606.png\" alt=\"01600606.png\"></figure><p>Select the damaged main bearing cap that was removed from the engine or another unserviceable main bearing cap that is appropriate for the engine and the main bearing location being repaired.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brwk.png\" alt=\"mb2brwk.png\"></figure><p>Remove any burrs from the joint face of the main bearing cap. A flat file wrapped in an emery cloth is effective.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600536.png\" alt=\"01600536.png\"></figure><p>The main bearing cap will be destroyed during the cutting operation. Use a scrap or damaged cap. <b>Do not</b> install a new cap at this time.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600537.png\" alt=\"01600537.png\"></figure><p>Install the unserviceable main bearing cap appropriate for the bore to be repaired.</p><p>Tighten the capscrews to specification. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600538.png\" alt=\"01600538.png\"></figure><p>The main bearing cap can be reused on other engines while making a saddle repair. It should be retained as part of the process tooling.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600541.png\" alt=\"01600541.png\"></figure><p>Install the remaining main bearing caps. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600539.png\" alt=\"01600539.png\"></figure><p>Use a dial indicator to align the center line of the main bearing bore with the center line of the boring bar.</p><p>The most accurate alignment is achieved from the undamaged main bearing bores that are at the ends of the block.</p><p>Correct alignment of the bores is ± 0.025 mm [± 0.001 in].</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600540.png\" alt=\"01600540.png\"></figure><p>Cummins Inc. recommends, for portable main bearing bore machining, the following tools are used for QK45/60 engines.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part Description</th><th>Part Number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mounting Adapter</td><td>3164879</td></tr><tr><td>Cutter Bits</td><td>3165027 and 3165028</td></tr><tr><td>Main Bearing Alignment Kit</td><td>3164874</td></tr><tr><td>Checking Bar/Boring Bar</td><td>3375061</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Reference the specific tool instructions for tool usage.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600542.png\" alt=\"01600542.png\"></figure><p>Clamp the cylinder block to the work table after correct alignment is achieved.</p><p>The cylinder block should <b>not</b> be permitted to move during the cutting operation.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600543.png\" alt=\"01600543.png\"></figure><p>Adjust the boring bar cutter to remove 0.25 to 0.51 mm [0.010 to 0.20 in] of material from the inside diameter of the damaged main bearing bore.</p><p>Reference the manufacturer&#x27;s instructions for cutter depth adjustment procedures.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600544.png\" alt=\"01600544.png\"></figure><p>Operate the cutter at 75 to 150 rpm. If the current tool tip wears quickly or breaks, reduce the speed of the cutter.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600545.png\" alt=\"01600545.png\"></figure><p>The amount of material that can be removed depends on the hardness of the surface material, type of boring machine, and the type of cutting tool being used.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600546.png\" alt=\"01600546.png\"></figure><p>Machine the bore to the following specifications.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Enlarged Main Bearing Bore Ready for Repair Sleeve Insert</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>195.40</td><td>MIN</td><td>7.693</td></tr><tr><td>195.50</td><td>MAX</td><td>7.697</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The final surface finish maximum specification is 2.03 µm [80 µin].</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600547.png\" alt=\"01600547.png\"></figure><p>Complete the cutting operation on each main bearing bore before proceeding to the adjacent bore(s). This lessens the potential for misalignment between adjacent main bearing bores.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600548.png\" alt=\"01600548.png\"></figure><p>Clean the main bearing bore(s) to remove cutting oils and metallic fines.</p><p>Spray cleaner, Part Number 3375433, or equivalent can be used.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brwu.png\" alt=\"mb2brwu.png\"></figure><p>Inspect the enlarged main bearing bore for any fault indication that was <b>not</b> discovered during the initial inspection. This is especially critical in instances where the bore was distressed by heat or impact loading.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600549.png\" alt=\"01600549.png\"></figure><p>After the cutting operation is complete, use a rigid or flexible hone to remove any burrs from each of the main bearing bores.</p><p>Remove all burrs from the oil supply hole drillings.</p><p>The surface finish <b>must</b> remain within the specification shown above.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600550.png\" alt=\"01600550.png\"></figure><p>Check the enlarged bearing bore(s) to be sure they are the correct size for the repair sleeve.</p><p>Measure each in four equally spaced directions.</p><p>The diameter of the enlarged main bearing bore(s) should <b>not</b> vary more than ± 0.05 mm [± 0.002 in].</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600551.png\" alt=\"01600551.png\"></figure><p>If the bore size is within specifications, proceed with the repair process.</p><p>If <b>not</b>, repair the main bore.</p><p>Consult the Alternative Repair Manual, Bulletin Number 3379035.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600552.png\" alt=\"01600552.png\"></figure><p>Remove the damaged main bearing cap(s) from the bore(s) that were cut for repair sleeves. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600553.png\" alt=\"01600553.png\"></figure><p>Prepare the enlarged bore for installation of the repair sleeve.</p><p>Clean the main bearing saddle area with spray cleaners, Part Numbers 3375433 and ST-1272-13, or equivalent.</p><p>Allow 2 minutes for the excess cleaner to evaporate.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brwu.png\" alt=\"mb2brwu.png\"></figure><p>Clean the outside diameter of the repair sleeve.</p><p>Use spray cleaner, Part Numbers 3375433, ST-1272-13, or equivalent, to remove any oils, grease, or preservative coating.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600526.png\" alt=\"01600526.png\"></figure><p>Align the main bearing locking tang slot in the repair sleeve with the locking tang slot in the cylinder block.</p><p>The oil hole in the repair sleeve <b>must</b> be centered over the oil hole in the block.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brwv.png\" alt=\"mb2brwv.png\"></figure><p>The ends of the repair sleeve should protrude above the main bearing bore joint faces.</p><p>In most cases, the protrusion will be approximately 1.52 mm [0.060 in] above the joint face of the cylinder block on each end of the repair sleeve.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01200024.png\" alt=\"01200024.png\"></figure><p>Check the side-to-side position of the repair sleeve. In most cases, the sleeve will be flush with the sides of the main bearing saddle.</p><p>A variance of ± 0.38 mm [± 0.015 in] from being flush with the saddle sidewall is acceptable.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb9slwb.png\" alt=\"mb9slwb.png\"></figure><p>Check the location and position of the oil supply holes.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brww.png\" alt=\"mb2brww.png\"></figure><p>Select two main bearing cap retaining capscrews.</p><p>Install one of the spacers which is part of the main bearing saddle hardware kit on to each of these capscrews, so that approximately 86.2 mm [3 in] of the capscrew protrudes beyond the end of the spacer.</p><p>Consult the Service Products Catalog, Bulletin 3377710.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600554.png\" alt=\"01600554.png\"></figure><p>Install a flat washer or the locking plate on to each of the capscrew and spacer assemblies. These locking plates are part of the main bearing saddle hardware kit. Consult the Service Products Catalog, Bulletin 3377710.</p><p>Install the capscrew assemblies in the cylinder block.</p><p>Position the locking washer so that the edge of the repair sleeve is held down by the flat washer or locking plate.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600555.png\" alt=\"01600555.png\"></figure><p>Tighten the capscrews and washer assemblies finger-tight.</p><p>Alternately tighten each of the capscrew assemblies.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>55 n•m [41 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600556.png\" alt=\"01600556.png\"></figure><p>A 6 mm [1/4 in] capacity drill motor and a 90 degree (right angle) drill is required for most Vee design engines, due to the position of the retaining rivets.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600557.png\" alt=\"01600557.png\"></figure><p>Install a 3.175 mm [1/8 in] diameter drill bit in the drill chuck.</p><p>Install a drill stop on the drill so the drilled depth is controlled to between 5.85 to 6.35 mm [0.230 to 0.250 in].</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb9togb.png\" alt=\"mb9togb.png\"></figure><p>The correct drill depth setting is <b>very important</b> for making a successful repair.</p><p>If the drill depth is too shallow or too deep, the rivet will <b>not</b> hold effectively.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600552.png\" alt=\"01600552.png\"></figure><p>Drill the cylinder block to accept the retaining rivets. Use the holes in the repair sleeve to determine the location and quantity of holes required.</p><p>Be sure to drill all the required rivet holes.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600559.png\" alt=\"01600559.png\"></figure><p>Evacuate shavings and other debris from the drilled holes.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/bp900ec.png\" alt=\"bp900ec.png\"></figure><p>Check the position of the drill stop to be sure the original depth setting is still correct.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600560.png\" alt=\"01600560.png\"></figure><p>If the drill bit has moved, be sure the drilled holes will permit the rivet to protrude approximately 2.54 mm [0.100 in] above the surface of the repair sleeve.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600558.png\" alt=\"01600558.png\"></figure><p>Select the correct quantity of retaining rivets.</p><p>The rivets come as part of a kit with the salvage sleeves. Consult the Service Products Catalog, Bulletin 3377710.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600561.png\" alt=\"01600561.png\"></figure><p>Put a small amount of sealant, Loctite™ 601, or equivalent, on the tip and body of the rivet.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600562.png\" alt=\"01600562.png\"></figure><p>Push the rivet through one of the drilled holes in the repair sleeve and into the drilled hole in the cylinder block.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600563.png\" alt=\"01600563.png\"></figure><p>The rivet should protrude above the surface of the repair sleeve by approximately 2.54 mm [0.100 in] when correctly installed.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600564.png\" alt=\"01600564.png\"></figure><p>Prepare and install the remaining rivets.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600565.png\" alt=\"01600565.png\"></figure><p>Peen the rivets to hold the repair sleeve in place.</p><p>Use the ball end of a 0.9 kg [2 lb] hammer (approximate weight and size) for the peening operation.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb9slwc.png\" alt=\"mb9slwc.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The repair sleeve can be fractured if hit during the peening process.</p></div></div><p>Do <b>not</b> use <b>any</b> fractured repair sleeves.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600566.png\" alt=\"01600566.png\"></figure><p>The retaining rivets <b>must</b> be fully peened. Do <b>not</b> peen excessively.</p><p>Excessive peening will result in rupturing or splitting of the rivet head.</p><p>A uniform peening pattern provides the best results.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb9slwd.png\" alt=\"mb9slwd.png\"></figure><p>Remove the retaining capscrews, spacer, and washer assemblies.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600567.png\" alt=\"01600567.png\"></figure><p>Cut the ends and sides of the repair sleeve flush with the main bearing cap joint face of the cylinder block and the sides of the main bearing bore saddle.</p><p>A 203.2 mm [8 in] mill file can be used to perform this operation.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brw03.png\" alt=\"mb2brw03.png\"></figure><p>Cut the edge(s) of the repair sleeve flush with the sides of the main bearing bore saddle.</p><p>A 203.2 mm [8 in] mill file can be used to perform this operation.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb9slwe.png\" alt=\"mb9slwe.png\"></figure><p>Clean the main bearing saddle and the main bearing capscrew threads.</p><p>Flush the threads with cleaner, Part Number 3375433, to remove filing fines.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/gh9cseb.png\" alt=\"gh9cseb.png\"></figure><p>Remove the filing fines and excess cleaner from the main bearing capscrew holes.</p><p>A chip removing unit, Part Number ST-1272-11, can be used for this operation.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600568.png\" alt=\"01600568.png\"></figure><p>Select the appropriate new replacement semi-finished main bearing cap or a good used main bearing cap.</p><p>Be sure the cap is the correct one for the engine and main bearing location.</p><p>Reference the Cylinder Block option in the parts catalog for the engine to determine the correct main bearing cap part number.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brw04.png\" alt=\"mb2brw04.png\"></figure><p>Check to be sure the replacement cap has the correct fit in the cylinder block press fit area(s).</p><p>Reference the Main Bearing Bore in the Inspect section in this procedure.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brw08.png\" alt=\"mb2brw08.png\"></figure><p>A used main bearing cap in good condition can be used.</p><p>The thickness of the parting face of the main bearing joint face <b>must</b> usually be reduced by 0.25 mm [0.010 in].</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600569.png\" alt=\"01600569.png\"></figure><p>Do <b>not</b> cut excessive amounts from the joint face of the main bearing cap.</p><p>Excessive cutting will <b>not</b> allow installation of the main bearing side bolts and/or correct installation of the main bearing cap retaining screws.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600570.png\" alt=\"01600570.png\"></figure><p>It is very important that the joint faces of the cap are cut parallel to each other and square to their mating surfaces in the cylinder block.</p><p>A high quality main bearing cap cutting machine <b>must</b> be used to cut the joint face of the cap.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brw05.png\" alt=\"mb2brw05.png\"></figure><p>The main bearing cap <b>must</b> fit squarely on the cylinder block&#x27;s joint face.</p><p>Main bearing caps that do <b>not</b> fit correctly can change assembly clearances and tolerances.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600571.png\" alt=\"01600571.png\"></figure><p>Check the squareness of the mating surface of the main bearing cap.</p><p>The joint faces of the cap <b>must</b> sit squarely with the cylinder block joint face, as determined by a bearing bluing compound.</p><p>Apply a light coating of bluing to the cylinder block joint face.</p><p>Install the main bearing cap.</p><p>Tighten the capscrews to approximately:</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>135 n•m [100 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600572.png\" alt=\"01600572.png\"></figure><p>Remove the retaining capscrews and the main bearing cap. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</p><p>The bluing should have transferred to the joint face of the main bearing cap.</p><p>A bluing transfer of less than 80 percent of the area of the joint face(s) is unacceptable.</p><p><b>Only</b> use main bearing caps that have a bluing transfer of 80 percent or greater.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brw07.png\" alt=\"mb2brw07.png\"></figure><p>Remove the bluing from the joint faces and main bearing bore of the main cylinder.</p><p>Use a soft cloth and spray cleaner, Part Number 3375433, or equivalent.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600573.png\" alt=\"01600573.png\"></figure><p>Install the new replacement main bearing cap. Use the following procedure for capscrew torques. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600539.png\" alt=\"01600539.png\"></figure><p>Mark the replacement cap to identify its location and position.</p><p>Steel stamps with 6.35 mm [1/4 in] characters are recommended.</p><p>Be sure to mark the cap in the same area as the other main bearing caps.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600513.png\" alt=\"01600513.png\"></figure><p>Use a dial indicator to align the center line of the main bearing bore with the center line of the boring bore.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600540.png\" alt=\"01600540.png\"></figure><p>The most accurate alignment is achieved from the undamaged main bearing bores that are the furthest from the damaged bores. These are usually the bores that are at the ends of the cylinder block.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600540.png\" alt=\"01600540.png\"></figure><p>Adjust the boring bar cutter to remove 0.25 to 0.51 mm [0.010 to 0.020 in] of material from the inside diameter of the main bearing bore.</p><p>Reference the manufacturer&#x27;s instructions for cutter depth adjustment procedures.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600544.png\" alt=\"01600544.png\"></figure><p>Operate the cutter at 75 to 150 rpm.</p><p>If the cutting tool tip wears quickly or breaks, reduce the speed of the cutter.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600545.png\" alt=\"01600545.png\"></figure><p>The amount of material that can be removed will depend on the following.</p><ul><li>Material harness</li><li>Amount the rivet protrudes from the surface of the repair sleeve</li><li>Type of boring machine</li><li>The surface finish requirements.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600574.png\" alt=\"01600574.png\"></figure><p>After cutting the main bearing bore the first time, approximately 1.52 mm [0.060 in] of material can be removed from the bore diameter with each additional cut.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brw21.png\" alt=\"mb2brw21.png\"></figure><p>After making the second cut, check the accuracy of the cutting tool by comparing the cut diameter of the main bearing bore with the set diameter of the cutting tool.</p><p>This size comparison will reduce the chances of error in setting the cutting tool and allow the bore to be cut to the proper finished size.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600575.png\" alt=\"01600575.png\"></figure><p>The surface finish <b>must</b> remain within the specification shown above.</p><p>Reduce the depth of the cut and reduce the cutter speed of feed if the surface finish is <b>not</b> acceptable.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/kn9towb.png\" alt=\"kn9towb.png\"></figure><p>Continue to cut the inside diameter of the main bearing bore until it is 0.13 mm [0.005 in] smaller than the required size.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600547.png\" alt=\"01600547.png\"></figure><p>Remove the remaining 0.13 mm [0.005 in] of material from the main bearing bore. Use reduced depth of cutting operations.</p><p>Check the cut diameter to be sure it is within new bore specifications. Refer to the Main Bearing Bore information in the Inspect section of this procedure.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brw21.png\" alt=\"mb2brw21.png\"></figure><p>Complete the cutting operation on each main bearing bore before proceeding to adjacent bore(s). This lessens the potential for misalignment between adjacent bearing bores.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600548.png\" alt=\"01600548.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The main bearing bore must be within specification tolerances. Engine damage will result if bores are out of tolerance.</p></div></div><p>Measure the newly cut bore diameter. Be sure it is within the stated size tolerance. Refer to the Main Bearing Bore information in the Inspect section of this procedure.</p><p>If the bore is too small, it <b>must</b> be machined to make the bore larger.</p><p>If the bore is too large, remove the repair sleeve and begin the main bearing saddle repair process again.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600604.png\" alt=\"01600604.png\"></figure><p>After the cutting operation is complete, use a rigid or flexible hone to remove any burrs from each main bearing bore.</p><p>The surface finish <b>must</b> remain within the specifications shown above.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600550.png\" alt=\"01600550.png\"></figure><p>Chamfer the oil supply hole in the repair sleeve to remove any burrs.</p><p>The chamfer angle <b>must</b> be between 82 and 100 degrees included angles.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01200025.png\" alt=\"01200025.png\"></figure><p>Make sure the oil supply holes in the cylinder block and main bearing bore repair sleeve are aligned.</p><p>If the holes are <b>not</b> aligned, the repair sleeve <b>must</b> be removed and the main bearing bore repair procedure <b>must</b> be started again.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/mb2brw23.png\" alt=\"mb2brw23.png\"></figure><p>Check the alignment of the main bores for short bend alignment.</p><p>Short bend alignment is the alignment between adjacent main bearing bores.</p><p>Pass a solid bar 0.025 mm [0.001 in] smaller than the main bearing bore inside diameter through the bores of the cylinder block.</p><p>A main bearing alignment kit can also be used. Reference the service tool instructions for information on how to use the bearing alignment kit.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600576.png\" alt=\"01600576.png\"></figure><p>Remove the main bearing caps. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600577.png\" alt=\"01600577.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids, or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><p>Brush the main bearing oil supply drillings to remove shavings and other debris.</p><p>Use solvent and a nylon brush for this operation.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600578.png\" alt=\"01600578.png\"></figure><p>Head Deck Surface</p><p>Resurface the cylinder block, if required, after the main bearing bores have been machined, and before machining the counterbore ledge depth.</p><p>This machine process can <b>only</b> be done on a milling machine with a machine bed large enough to hold the entire cylinder block.</p><p>Do <b>not</b> remove too much material. This will result in piston to valve contact.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck1bdxa.png\" alt=\"ck1bdxa.png\"></figure><p>Determine how much material is to be removed before machining, to determine which oversize head gasket should be used. Reference the block deck height specifications above.</p><p>Machine the cylinder block head deck surface. Use the appropriate method for the milling machine being used to achieve the desired results.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600579.png\" alt=\"01600579.png\"></figure><p>Check the new surface from end-to-end to determine the surface parallelism.</p><p>The surface should <b>not</b> vary more than 0.0375 mm [0.0015 in] for each 508 mm [20 in] of the cylinder block surface length.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600580.png\" alt=\"01600580.png\"></figure><p>Check the new surface from side-to-side to determine surface perpendicularity.</p><p>The surface should <b>not</b> vary more than 0.02 mm [0.001 in] in 254 mm [10 in] of the cylinder block surface width.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600581.png\" alt=\"01600581.png\"></figure><p>Check for discoloration, heat checks, or heat spots on the top deck surface. These deck conditions are <b>not</b> acceptable as a finished surface condition.</p><p>If the maximum amount of material has been removed and the block shows signs of discoloration and heat spots, check the cylinder block hardness at these positions. If the hardness is below 205HB, the block can <b>not</b> be reused.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600582.png\" alt=\"01600582.png\"></figure><p>The following are the specifications for cylinder block head deck surface finish.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cylinder Block Head Deck Surface Finish</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>micro-m</td><td></td><td>micro-in</td></tr><tr><td>2.03</td><td>MIN</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>3.18</td><td>MAX</td><td>125</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600583.png\" alt=\"01600583.png\"></figure><p>Reference the parts catalog to find information on the correct oversize head gasket to be used.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600584.png\" alt=\"01600584.png\"></figure><p>Mark the cylinder block to indicate the thickness of the head gasket that <b>must</b> be used with the machined cylinder block.</p><p>The preferred location to mark the cylinder block is on the left front top surface.</p><p>Use 1/4 inch characters. The common notation is STD.; 0.010, 0.020 for either the standard or oversize gaskets.</p><p>Be sure to remove any burrs caused by the stamping operation.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600585.png\" alt=\"01600585.png\"></figure><p>Measure the concentricity of each of the cylinder bores before you remove the cylinder block from the machine work surface.</p><p>Use the following procedure for cylinder bore concentricity specifications and machining processes. Refer to Procedure 001-027 in Section 1.</p><p>If any bores are outside of the specifications, mark them for repair.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck8gavd.png\" alt=\"ck8gavd.png\"></figure><p>Measure each counterbore depth and compare with the specifications.</p><p>After the cylinder block head deck has been machined, all cylinder counterbores <b>must</b> be machined to the correct cylinder bore specifications.</p><p>Use the following procedure for cylinder bore concentricity specifications and machining processes. Refer to Procedure 001-027 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck4brta.png\" alt=\"ck4brta.png\"></figure><p>Measure the depth (position) of the lower liner bore entry chamfer from the top deck surface.</p><p>After machining the cylinder block head deck surface, all cylinder lower liner bore entry chamfers will require machining to bring the cylinder block back to original specifications.</p><p>Use the following procedure for cylinder bore concentricity specifications and machining processes. Refer to Procedure 001-027 in Section 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600605.png\" alt=\"01600605.png\"></figure><p>Depending on the amount of material removed from the head deck surface, the edge of the liner catchers may require machining.</p><p>Measure the distance between the edge of the liner catchers and the cylinder block head deck surface.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Distance Between Liner Catchers and Cylinder Block Head Deck Surface</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>288.900</td><td>MIN</td><td>11.374</td></tr><tr><td>289.100</td><td>MAX</td><td>11.382</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600627.png\" alt=\"01600627.png\"></figure><p>Clean the block ready for reuse as described earlier in this procedure.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the cylinder block is <b>not</b> to be used immediately, spray a rust preventive solution on the block and cover the cylinder block to protect it from the environment.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600512.png\" alt=\"01600512.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Machining of the left bank pan rail inner wall can only be done on QSK60 cylinder blocks. Do not attempt this operation on QSK45 cylinder blocks as engine damage could possibly occur.</p></div></div><p>Pan Rail:</p><p>The QSK60 left bank pan rail inner wall can be machined at the main bearing cap location. The machine cuts allow for removal of the main bearing caps without the need to remove the crankshaft counterweights and installation of the main bearing caps without the need to remove the crankshaft counterweights or rotate the crankshaft.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Some QSK60 engines may already have this feature machined in to the left bank pan rail inner wall.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600643.png\" alt=\"01600643.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Do not machine main bearing cap locations 1, 7, and 9 as engine damage could possibly occur.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Only the left bank pan rail inner wall can be machined. Do not machine the right bank pan rail inner wall or both pan rail inner walls as engine damage could possibly occur.</p></div></div><p>The machine cuts <b>mustonly</b> be done at main bearing cap locations 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 8.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600653.png\" alt=\"01600653.png\"></figure><p>Machine the left bank pan rail inner wall in the main bearing cap locations shown in the illustration to the following dimensions and specifciation.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Left Bank Pan Rail Inner Wall Dimensions</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>mm</td><td></td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>109.25</td><td>MIN</td><td>4.301</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>109.75</td><td>MAX</td><td>4.321</td></tr><tr><td>Y</td><td>16.75</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.659</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>17.25</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.679</td></tr><tr><td>R</td><td>4.55</td><td>MIN</td><td>0.179</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5.05</td><td>MAX</td><td>0.199</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Measurements</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>micro-m</td><td>in</td></tr><tr><td>Surface Finish</td><td>3.2</td><td>0.0001</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600654.png\" alt=\"01600654.png\"></figure><p>Clean the block ready for reuse as described earlier in this procedure.</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the cylinder block is <b>not</b> to be used immediately, spray a rust preventive solution on the block and cover the cylinder block to protect it from the environment.</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01600512.png\" alt=\"01600512.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><ul><li>Install the cylinder liners. Refer to Procedure 001-028 in Section 1.</li><li>Install the crankshaft. Refer to Procedure 001-016 in Section 1.</li><li>Install the gear housing. Use the following procedure in the QSK45 and QSK60 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021530\">4021530</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/56-001-033-tr\">Refer to Procedure 001-033 in Section 1.</a> Use the following procedure in the Industrial QSK45G and QSK60G Series Engines Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin 4021383, and the QSK60G Generator CM700 Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin 4021396. Refer to Procedure 001-033 in Section 1.</li><li>Install the cylinder head. Refer to Procedure 002-004 in Section 2.</li><li>Install the engine. Refer to Procedure 000-002 in Section 0.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure>","102-005-232":"<h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Depending on the circumstance, diesel fuel is flammable. When inspecting or performing service or repairs on the fuel system, to reduce the possibility of fire and resulting severe personal injury, death or property damage, never smoke or allow sparks or flames (such as pilot lights, arcing equipment, or welding equipment) in the work area.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids, or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacture&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause bodily injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A very small amount of dirt and debris can be very harmful to the fuel pump. Extra care is required to keep the fuel connections clean during removal and installation. Connections must be covered immediately to keep them clean when components are removed from the fuel pump.</p></div></div><ul><li>Disconnect the battery cables. See the equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Clean the fuel pump and the surrounding area. Dry with compressed air.</li><li>Dry with compressed air.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure><h3>Remove</h3><p>Disconnect the wiring harness connector (1) from the fuel pump pressurizing assembly.</p><p>Remove the three inner capscrews (2) and the fuel pump pressurizing assembly.</p><p>Discard the o-rings.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05600383.png\" alt=\"05600383.png\"></figure><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Remove <b>only</b> the adapter plate, if necessary. If removing, mark the orientation of the plate so that it can be installed in the same orientation. This will make sure that the wiring harness connector will be positioned in the correct location when the fuel pump pressurizing assembly is installed.</p></div></div><p>To remove the adapter plate, remove the four outer capscrews (1) and pull out the adapter plate.</p><p>Discard the o-rings.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05600384.png\" alt=\"05600384.png\"></figure><h3>Inspect for Reuse</h3><p>Inspect the fuel pump pressurizing assembly for damage.</p><p>Replace, the assembly if damage is found.</p><p>Check the resistance of the fuel pump pressuring assembly. Use the following procedure in the Troubleshooting and Repair Manual Electronic Control System, QSK50 and QSK60 Modular Common Rail System Series Engines, Bulletin 4021533. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/99-019-360\">Refer to Procedure 019-360 in Section 19.</a></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05600222.png\" alt=\"05600222.png\"></figure><h3>Resistance Check</h3><p>Disconnect the engine wiring harness from the fuel pump pressurizing assembly.</p><p>Measure the resistance from the SIGNAL pin and all other pins in the connector. The multimeter <b>must</b> measure between 1 and 10 ohms or above 100k ohms. If the resistance values are within specifications, the connector <b>must</b> still be checked for a short to ground.</p><p>Measure the resistance from the SIGNAL pin and ground. The multimeter <b>must</b> measure between 1 and 10 ohms.</p><p>If any of the preceding checks fail, check each harness connected in series to determine which one contains the open circuit. Repair or replace the damaged section of the engine harness. If no harness issues are found, replace the fuel pump pressurizing assembly.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05400247.png\" alt=\"05400247.png\"></figure><h3>Install</h3><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>This step block <b>only</b> applies if the adapter plate has been removed. The adapter plate should <b>only</b> be removed if necessary.</p></div></div><p>Install the adapter plate onto the fuel pump and the four outer capscrews (1), using new o-rings. Make sure that the adapter plate is oriented so that when the fuel pump pressurizing assembly is installed, the wiring harness connector will be in the correct location.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>8 n•m [71 in-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05600384.png\" alt=\"05600384.png\"></figure><p>Install the fuel pump pressurizing assembly with new o-rings and the three inner capscrews (2).</p><p>Tighten the capscrews.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Fuel pump pressurizing capscrews 6.5 n•m [58 in-lb]</p></div></div><p>Connect the wiring harness to the fuel pump pressurizing assembly (1).</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05600383.png\" alt=\"05600383.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (-) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Connect the batteries. See the equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Operate the engine and check for leaks.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure>","102-006-061":"<h3>General Information</h3><p>This procedure applies <b>only</b> to engines equipped with electronically controlled injectors.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/06k00003.png\" alt=\"06k00003.png\"></figure><h3>Fuel Pressure Relief Valve Return Flow Test</h3><p>Obtain a graduated cylinder that is marked in cubic centimeters; example: graduated cylinder, Part Number 4919139, or equivalent.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05944490.png\" alt=\"05944490.png\"></figure><p>Remove the fuel pressure relief valve drain line from the fuel drain manifold.</p><p>Install an M18 plug in the fuel drain manifold.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/06e00096.png\" alt=\"06e00096.png\"></figure><p>Route the fuel pressure relief valve drain line into a graduated cylinder.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/06f00007.png\" alt=\"06f00007.png\"></figure><p>Connect Cummins® electronic service tool or equivalent, start engine, and operate the Fuel System Leakage Test.</p><p>See Cummins® electronic service tool or equivalent manual for instructions.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/19c00691.png\" alt=\"19c00691.png\"></figure><p>Leakage <b>must</b> be less than 10 drops per minute.</p><p>A steady leak from the fuel pressure relief valve can be an indication that the valve seat is worn.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08c00214.png\" alt=\"08c00214.png\"></figure><p>Install the fuel pressure relief valve drain line into the fuel drain manifold.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Banjo Capscrews 48 n•m [35 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/06400437.png\" alt=\"06400437.png\"></figure><h3>Preparatory Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (1) battery cable last.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Depending on the circumstance, diesel fuel is flammable. When inspecting or performing service or repairs on the fuel system, to reduce the possibility of fire and resulting severe personal injury, death or property damage, never smoke or allow sparks or flames (such as pilot lights, electrical switches, or welding equipment) in the work area.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids, or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Wear appropriate eye and face protection when using compressed air. Flying debris and dirt can cause personal injury.</p></div></div><div class=\"callout warning\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>CAUTION · Осторожно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A very small amount of dirt and debris can be very harmful to the fuel pump. Extra care is required to keep the fuel connections clean during removal and installation. Connections must be covered immediately to keep them clean when components are removed from the fuel pump.</p></div></div><ul><li>Disconnect the battery cables. See equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>Clean the fuel pump and surrounding area, and dry with compressed air.</li><li>Disconnect the fuel drain line from the fuel pump. Use a clean plastic plug to protect the fuel drain line port.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure><h3>Remove</h3><p>The fuel pump is equipped with metric fasteners. Use metric size tools for the removal and installation of the valve.</p><p>Remove the mechanical dump valve from the fuel pump. Discard the o-ring.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05400260.png\" alt=\"05400260.png\"></figure><h3>Clean and Inspect for Reuse</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When using solvents, acids, or alkaline materials for cleaning, follow the manufacturer&#x27;s recommendations for use. Wear goggles and protective clothing to reduce the possibility of personal injury.</p></div></div><p>Clean all parts with electrical contact cleaner, Part Number 3824510, or equivalent.</p><p>Inspect the mechanical dump valve for stripped threads, damage, restricted passage, or cracks. Replace the valve, if damaged.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05400269.png\" alt=\"05400269.png\"></figure><h3>Install</h3><p>Install the new o-ring on the mechanical dump valve.</p><p>Install the mechanical dump valve in the fuel pump.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>135 n•m [100 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05400260.png\" alt=\"05400260.png\"></figure><h3>Finishing Steps</h3><div class=\"callout danger\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">⚠</span>WARNING · Опасно</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Batteries can emit explosive gases. To reduce the possibility of personal injury, always ventilate the compartment before servicing the batteries. To reduce the possibility of arcing, remove the negative (-) battery cable first and attach the negative (1) battery cable last.</p></div></div><ul><li>Install the fuel return line.</li><li>Connect the battery cables. See equipment manufacturer service information.</li></ul><p>Start and operate the engine and check for leaks.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure>","102-007-066":"<h3>Disassemble</h3><p>For a detailed Disassemble procedure, use the Maintenance Section in the Eliminator™ Filter, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/3666253\">3666253</a>.</p><h3>Clean and Inspect for Reuse</h3><p>For a detailed Clean and Inspect for Reuse, use the Maintenance Section in the Eliminator™ Filter, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/3666253\">3666253</a>.</p><h3>Assemble</h3><p>For a detailed Assemble procedure, use the Maintenance Section in the Eliminator™ Filter, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/3666253\">3666253</a>.</p><h3>Pressure Test</h3><p>This test will indicate oil pressure before and after the oil flows through the Eliminator™.</p><p>Use a differential pressure gauge or one pressure gauge with two oil hoses and two valves (1 and 2) to eliminate gauge error. Use a gauge with a minimum pressure capacity of 1400 kPa [200 psi].</p><p>Be sure the engine is switched off.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07e00184.png\" alt=\"07e00184.png\"></figure><p>Remove the two plugs from the filter housing.</p><p>Install the 3end of the valve assembly in the port of the filter nearest to the centrifuge from where the plug was removed.</p><p>Install the end of the valve assembly in the port of the filter furthest away from the centrifuge from where the other plug has been removed.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07600238.png\" alt=\"07600238.png\"></figure><p>Operate the engine at rated revolutions per minute (rpm) until the engine oil reaches its normal operating temperature. Continue running the engine at rated rpm for the duration of the pressure test.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/oi400la.png\" alt=\"oi400la.png\"></figure><p>Close valve (1), open valve (2), and record the pressure value before the Elininator™.</p><p>Close valve (2), open valve (1), and record the pressure value after the Eliminator™.</p><p>If the difference in pressure is more than the maximum allowable value, inspect and clean the Eliminator™ screens before checking the oil pressure differential again.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Measurements</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>kPa</td><td>psi</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum Oil Pressure Differential</td><td>60</td><td>9</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07e00184.png\" alt=\"07e00184.png\"></figure><p>Turn the engine OFF.</p><p>Remove the valve assembly from the Eliminator™ and install the two plugs.</p><p>Tighten the plugs.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>10 n•m [89 in-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07600238.png\" alt=\"07600238.png\"></figure>","102-007-067":"<h3>General Information</h3><p>The following steps will be accomplished at different intervals. See the maintenance schedule in the Operation and Maintenance manual for Rotation Check and Pressure Test intervals for the engine being serviced.</p><ul><li>Use the following procedure for QSK19 engines. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/20-102-002-om\">Refer to Procedure 102-002 in Section 2.</a></li><li>Use the following procedure for QSK45 and QSK60 engines. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/56-102-002-om\">Refer to Procedure 102-002 in Section 2.</a></li><li>Use the following procedure for K38, K50, QSK38, and QSK50 engines. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-102-002-om\">Refer to Procedure 102-002 in Section 2.</a></li><li>Use the following procedure for QSK50 CM2350 K108 engines. Refer to Procedure 102-002 in Section 2.</li><li>Use the following procedure for QSK78 engines. Refer to Procedure 102-002 in Section 2.</li><li>Use the following procedure for QSK78 CM2220 K104 engines. Refer to Procedure 102-002 in Section 2.</li><li>Use the following procedure for QSK60 CM2520 K112 engines. Refer to Procedure 102-002 in Section 2.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ck800wa.png\" alt=\"ck800wa.png\"></figure><h3>Pressure Test</h3><p>Perform the following steps to measure the pressure drop across the Eliminator™ Filter.</p><p>Remove one plug from each side at the top of the filter housing.</p><p>Install the pressure gauges in the ports.</p><p>Operate the engine to normal operating temperature.</p><p>Record the readings from both gauges.</p><p>The difference between the two measurements is the pressure drop across the filter.</p><p>If the difference in pressure is more than the maximum allowable value, refer to Service Bulletin 3666253 before checking the oil pressure differential again.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Maximum Oil Pressure Differential</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>kpa</td><td></td><td>psi</td></tr><tr><td>60</td><td>MAX</td><td>9</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Remove the pressure gauges.</p><p>Install and tighten the plugs.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>10 n•m [89 in-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07600238.png\" alt=\"07600238.png\"></figure><h3>Rotation Check</h3><p>To provide continued reliable operation, the filter <b>must</b> be checked at regular intervals.</p><p>The operating indicator is used to check the operation of the hydraulic motor.</p><p>Check the operating indicator daily at low idle condition. Refer to Service Bulletin 3666253. If the indicator makes one revolution between 4 and 10 minutes, the Eliminator™ hydraulic motor is operating properly. If it does <b>not</b> make the revolution in this time period, the hydraulic motor will need to be replaced. Contact a Cummins® Authorized Repair Location.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07600040.png\" alt=\"07600040.png\"></figure>"};
